    thinking: "**Designing the Repository**\n\nI\'ve finished the `SyncPreferences` class. Now, I\'m shifting focus to `ObsidianSyncRepository`. I\'ve started designing it. I\'m going to write `ObsidianSyncRepository.kt`. I\'m considering what logic it should contain. I\'m planning it to take `Context` (or `SyncPreferences` and `TaskDao`).\n\n\n**Defining the Sync Function**\n\nI\'m now deep in the logic for `syncTasks()`. I\'ve outlined the core steps. First, the mode selection, then URL construction, and finally the GET request. For Markdown mode, I\'ve got a regex for parsing task lines, extracting the completion status and task text. The next part will be constructing the `Task` entity, including determining its unique `id` using a combination of line index and content hash.\n\n\n**Refining Task Storage**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on task storage. I\'m finalizing the structure of the `Task` entity, including `isCompleted`, `notePath`, `lineNumber`, and `rawMarkdownLine`. I\'m going to implement a `clearTasks()` and `insertTasks(newList)` transaction within Room.  I\'m now deep in the details of handling JSON mode, exploring manual parsing and library options.\n\n\n**Implementing JSON Parsing**\n\nI\'ve decided to leverage `org.json` for flexible JSON parsing, using `JSONArray` and `JSONObject`. This approach provides built-in robustness for handling dynamic fields. I\'m focusing on common keys like \"text\", \"title\", and \"content\", along with variations for completion status such as \"completed,\" \"done,\" or \"checked\". I\'m also including a fallback of \"Task i\" for missing text fields.\n\n\n**Crafting the Toggle Logic**\n\nI\'m now deeply engrossed in designing the toggle functionality. For Markdown mode, I\'m outlining the process. I\'ll need to fetch the file content, split it into lines, and then replace the appropriate line based on the task\'s line number. I\'m considering using either the database content or refetching the file. It appears fetching is preferable.\n\n\n**Defining Toggle Functionality**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the toggle functionality for Markdown mode, specifically the replacement logic. I\'ve formulated the core update method, focusing on toggling the completion status within the task line. My strategy involves a simple regex to swap brackets: `- [ ]` to `- [x]` or vice versa. The server-side update will then be a POST or PUT of the altered file content. Finally, I will also need to update the local database and notify the widget. In JSON mode, I\'m considering standard PUT/POST methods, including potentially configurable options to send an update, or call a dedicated toggle hook. I\'m leaning toward allowing either updating the full item JSON, or the hook, for maximum flexibility.\n\n\n**Implementing the Network Requests**\n\nI\'m now detailing the network requests. I\'ve decided to include robust logging for all requests. The user will know the exact URL, status code, and payload of any requests. The endpoint could be either `<endpoint>/<id>` (PUT) or a POST to `<endpoint>` or `<endpoint>/toggle`. The PUT method will support the full updated JSON object. If that fails, or to provide fallback, I\'ll allow a POST to the `<endpoint>/toggle` endpoint. I\'ll utilize OkHttp for these requests. I\'m also ensuring the widget updates promptly after data changes. I can either broadcast an update or call `AppWidgetManager`.\n\n\n"
