  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Task Structure**\n\nI\'m currently updating `Task.kt` to handle both category parsing and time ranges. My attention then shifted to understanding the user\'s focus block structure. For example, \"18:30 - 19:00\" maps to \"Learn Dark Necessities bass line.\" I\'m determining how to best integrate time and category features.\n\n\n**Implementing Time Integration**\n\nI\'ve decided to lean into the time-prefix detection. If a task\'s text begins with a time pattern (like \"18:30 - 19:00\"), I\'ll classify it as a \"FOCUS_BLOCK.\" This will allow me to easily extract the time range and display the task as designed, with time above and the task text below, in a clean vertical layout. The time range will be secondary in styling. This approach makes categorization and UI display much simpler and elegant.\n\n\n**Parsing Markdown Headings**\n\nI\'m now integrating Markdown heading parsing into `ObsidianSyncRepository.kt`. The goal is to classify tasks based on Markdown heading structures. I\'ll need to parse headings like \"## Focus Blocks\" and sub-headings like \"### Admin\" to assign categories and subcategories to tasks. This approach will gracefully handle both explicit time ranges and implicit, heading-based categorization.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will update the `Task` entity in `Task.kt` to include fields for time ranges, a cleaned display title, categories (such as FOCUS_BLOCK versus UNTIMED), and subcategories (derived from markdown headings). This allows us to replicate and present the precise Obsidian visual hierarchy displayed in the user\'s reference image!"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 325051923
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
