  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Obsidian Layouts**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the user\'s Obsidian structure. It appears they are segmenting their tasks into distinct blocks: focus periods, and untimed micro-tasks. The expanders under categories like \'Admin\' are particularly interesting. I\'m noting the custom section headers too, a crucial element of the overall methodology.\n\n\n**Examining Obsidian Parsing**\n\nI\'ve been drilling down into ObsidianSyncRepository.kt, specifically the parsing logic within parseAndSaveMarkdown. The taskRegex on line 124 is a key part. I realized I can elegantly capture section headers like \"FOCUS BLOCKS\" and \"FLOATING MICRO-TASKS\" to categorize checklist items. Subsequent headers like \"Admin\" or \"Work\" can be captured too. This sectioning is crucial, and it\'s quite elegant!\n\n\n**Extracting Time Ranges**\n\nI\'m now integrating time range extraction from task strings. My focus is on the `(\\d{1,2}:\\d{2}\\s*-\\s*\\d{1,2}:\\d{2})` regex. I\'m thinking of a structured parsing approach to separate the time range and display title. I\'ve designed a test Markdown sample to evaluate the extraction strategy across different formats. I\'m focusing on the elegant presentation of timed tasks, mirroring the visual style.\n\n\n**Implementing Date Handling**\n\nI\'ve incorporated logic to extract and store the page\'s heading, specifically for the date, such as \"Sat, Jun 13\". I plan to use it as the app/widget header, storing it in `SyncPreferences` as `lastSyncDateHeader` for authentication. Moreover, I\'m parsing the Obsidian content line-by-line, and I\'m capturing category and subcategory information. I\'m focusing on handling both timed and untimed tasks.\n\n\n**Refining Default Preferences**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on configuring default preferences for a smooth user experience. I\'m focusing on `SyncPreferences.kt` and noting the current server IP and port. My focus is on updating these defaults to match the observed user setup, setting the IP to `10.0.0.75` and the port to `8089`. This proactive measure aims for immediate usability for the user, removing the need for initial configuration.\n\n\n**Updating Default Preferences**\n\nI\'m now back in `SyncPreferences.kt`, and I\'ve verified I can set the default IP address to `10.0.0.75`. I\'m also now zeroing in on a complete plan, starting with model and domain updates, and then updating preferences, making the app immediately configured for the user. My focus is on making the app as usable as possible.\n\n\n**Designing Main App Interface**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the main app\'s look and feel, and my emphasis is on a Material 3 dark-themed UI to mirror Obsidian\'s aesthetic. I will include a real-time date and time header and the synchronized markdown header. The Config section will be single view. Finally, I will add a live Task List Board.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will inspect the existing UI theme color and theme configuration files (`Color.kt` and `Theme.kt`) to align the application\'s visual system with Obsidian\'s signature dark aesthetic: deep obsidian grays, refined charcoal cards, and a striking violet/purple accent."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 543623348
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
